values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i092$il$, $il$The drafting agent is reading unsent drafts as if they were sent messages$il$, $il$Outreach & drafting$il$, $il$Both$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-16T13:16:17.000Z'::timestamptz, 4, '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$Florian Pfeiffer, P706. The whole thread so far is ONE connection request, sent 28 August. Nothing else has gone out. The footer knows this and says logged as message 1 of 3.

The agent does not. The draft it has just written opens Hallo Florian, eine Sache noch zu dem Geraetekauf ohne Tarif. One more thing, to somebody who has received nothing.

The reason is visible directly underneath: PREVIOUS DRAFTS 2. A first message written 4 September and a follow up written 8 September, neither sent. The agent has read them as history and written a third touch on top.

THE FIX: drafts are drafts. They must be labelled as unsent and must never count as messages in the history the drafting agent reads, or in the sequence position.

Do not hide them from the model though. Showing prior drafts stops it regenerating the same thing twice. Show them clearly marked as UNSENT DRAFTS, never as part of the conversation.$il$,
$il$IT IS NOT COSMETIC. Eine Sache noch to a person who has heard nothing from us reads as though we have lost track of who we are talking to, on the very first real touch. That is the opposite of the impression the whole opener is designed to create, and it is worse in German where the continuation is unmistakable.

IT IS THE SECOND WAY THE SEQUENCE POSITION IS BEING CORRUPTED, from a different direction than i053. There, a duplicated touch row made the counter read chaser 3 of 2. Here, unsent drafts are inflating what the agent believes has been said. Both come from the same missing definition: nothing states clearly what counts as a touch. A touch is a message that left the building. Not a draft, not a revision, not a row that got written twice.

THE FOOTER IS ALREADY RIGHT, which is useful. It says message 1 of 3 and it is correct. So the sequence logic and the drafting context are reading two different things. Point them at the same source and this closes.

A DRAFT ON THIS SCREEN IS SIGNED BRAD. The 8 September regenerated version ends with the name Brad rather than Oliver. That is not the Oli variant in i067, it is a different person's name on Oliver's outbound, and it could have been sent. Widening i067 accordingly.

THE LANGUAGE ALSO MOVES WITHIN ONE THREAD. The 4 September draft is English, the 8 September one German, the current one German. Whatever decides language is not stable per contact. Minor next to the rest, but worth a look while the drafting context is being fixed, since it points the same way: the agent is not being given a consistent view of the thread.$il$);

