values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i093$il$, $il$Which channels are open is a different question from where the conversation is$il$, $il$Data & schema$il$, $il$Both$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-16T14:16:56.000Z'::timestamptz, 1, '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$Daniel Freudenberger at Rebuy cannot be sent a connection request: LinkedIn wants his email address first, because he has restricted who may invite him. Oliver asked whether Outreach Status should get a Blocked value, and then spotted the flaw himself. He might still email or call, so Blocked would say something untrue about the whole contact.

HE IS RIGHT, AND THE FIX IS NOT A NEW STATUS VALUE. Outreach Status answers where the conversation is: not started, ready, active, contacted, in conversation, cooldown. Channel availability answers something else entirely: which routes are open to this person. Putting one inside the other collapses two axes.

Record it per channel on the contact, with a reason:
  Connection request: available, or not available because LinkedIn requires their email
  InMail: available, and what it costs
  Email: available, no address on file, or not permitted in this market
  Phone: number on file or not

THEN IT STOPS BEING A DEAD END AND BECOMES A ROUTE. For this contact the conclusion follows automatically: the CR is closed, Rebuy is German so cold email is not permitted, which leaves InMail or the phone. The system can say that instead of leaving Oliver to work it out per contact.

Oliver suggested just listing it for now. I have made it a must because it feeds the next step logic and the connection request budget, both of which are live problems this week. Knock it back if you disagree.$il$,
$il$THIS IS THE THIRD TIME THE SAME MISTAKE HAS ALMOST BEEN MADE, which is why it is worth stating as a principle rather than fixing one field. Priority holds OoS, which is a status wearing a priority's clothes, see i041. Refurbished and SIM-free conflate condition with commercial model, see i086. And now a channel constraint is looking for a home inside a pipeline status. The rule: if two facts can be true independently, they are two fields. Oliver caught this one himself before it was built, which is the cheapest place to catch it.

IT FEEDS THE CONTACT LEVEL NEXT STEP, the one we sparred on and have not yet added. Send a connection request is the wrong next step for a person who cannot receive one. With channel availability recorded, the next step derives correctly on its own: CR unavailable, email not permitted, therefore InMail, one credit, or a call. Without it, the next step will keep proposing something impossible.

THERE MAY BE A QUIET SIGNAL IN IT WORTH USING. A profile that requires an email to receive an invitation is one whose owner has deliberately restricted invitations. Those people plausibly accept less often. With the daily allowance now rationed at twenty and the allowance itself keyed to acceptance rate, per i072, deprioritising them may be right regardless. Worth watching rather than assuming, but it argues for recording the flag rather than just working around it.

WHERE THE FLAG COMES FROM. Nobody should type this. It is learned at the moment Oliver tries and LinkedIn refuses, so the cleanest capture is at that point, either by hand in one click or through the extension in i045. A field that depends on somebody remembering to set it will be empty for most contacts and misleading for the rest.$il$);
