values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i094$il$, $il$Lovable says 1st degree and Accepted, LinkedIn says 2nd degree with the request still pending$il$, $il$Data & schema$il$, $il$Brad$il$, $il$blocker$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-17T09:40:09.000Z'::timestamptz, 1, '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${"Brad": "2026-09-20T14:40:50.058Z"}$il$::jsonb,
$il$Florian Pfeiffer, Head of Sales B2C at mobilezone Deutschland (Sparhandy), P706.

LOVABLE SAYS: Connection 1st degree, Connection Status Accepted. That implies a free direct message.

SALES NAVIGATOR SAYS: 2nd degree, Connection sent 8/28/2026, still pending. The timeline shows one event on 28 August, you sent Florian a connection request, and nothing since. There is no accept.

SO THE FREE DM DOES NOT EXIST. Oliver cannot message him without an InMail credit, and the system is telling him he can.

THE RECORD NAMES THE SOURCE OF THE BAD WRITE ITSELF. Next Action reads: CONNECTION STATUS CORRECTED, Request sent to Accepted, 2026-09-02, from Oliver's Sales Nav Recently Accepted Connections list. So something read that list on 2 September and flipped this contact to Accepted when he had not accepted. That means this is not a one-off typo. EVERY CONTACT TOUCHED BY THAT SAME IMPORT ON THAT DATE HAS TO BE RE-CHECKED, and we need to know how many there were.

THE RECORD IS ALSO SELF-CONTRADICTORY: Outreach Status reads To contact while Connection Status reads Accepted. Two fields describing the same person disagree, and nothing flagged it.

WHAT IS NEEDED
1. Correct this record: 2nd degree, connection request sent 2026-08-28, pending.
2. Find every record written by the 2026-09-02 Recently Accepted Connections import and re-verify each one against Sales Navigator.
3. Whatever wrote it must never again set Accepted without a positive accept signal. Absence from a list is not an accept.
4. Connection state needs a source and a date on it, so a wrong write can be traced and reversed without reading it out of a free text Next Action field.$il$,
$il$WHY THIS IS A BLOCKER AND NOT A DATA TIDY-UP. Connection degree is not a display field. Three things run off it: whether the DM is free or costs an InMail credit, which channel the next step proposes, and what the drafter assumes about the relationship. All three are wrong for this contact right now. With the daily connection request allowance rationed at twenty, see i072, and InMail credits finite, a field that says free when the answer is one credit spends real money.

SAME CONTACT AS i092. Florian Pfeiffer is also the record where two unsent drafts are being read as sent messages. So on one person the sequence thinks two messages have gone out and the channel logic thinks he is connected. Neither is true. Fixing one and not the other still leaves the record unusable, and it is worth Brad looking at both on this record together rather than separately.

HOW THE BAD WRITE PROBABLY HAPPENED. The Sales Nav Recently Accepted Connections list is a rolling view. If the importer treated presence in that view as accept and then wrote Accepted to everyone in the batch, or matched the wrong row, a pending invitation gets promoted. The safe version only ever moves a contact forward on an explicit accept event with its own date, and never infers state from a list membership.

RELATED: i053 covers touch identity, the same underlying problem of state being written without a verifiable event behind it. i093 covers channel availability as a separate axis from pipeline position, and this item is the argument for it, because a wrong degree quietly produces a wrong channel.

WHAT OLIVER IS DOING IN THE MEANTIME. Treating Lovable's connection state as unverified and checking Sales Nav before sending anything that depends on it. That is not sustainable across 130 leads, which is why it is a blocker.

EVIDENCE: two screenshots, the Lovable contact card and the Sales Navigator profile, both dated 17 September 2026.$il$);

