values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i096$il$, $il$Approve says it worked and the database says duplicate key, so nobody knows which approvals saved$il$, $il$Outreach & drafting$il$, $il$Brad$il$, $il$blocker$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-17T10:20:00.000Z'::timestamptz, 0, '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${"Brad": "2026-09-20T14:40:52.761Z"}$il$::jsonb,
$il$THREE INSTANCES ON 17 SEPTEMBER, ALL WITHIN A COUPLE OF HOURS.

Frederic Gandon at Largo, P566. Hartmut Baumann at Electronic Partner, P675. Leonard Coen at topi, P707. Every time, Approve returns a green Draft approved next to a red duplicate key value violates unique constraint draft_revisions_outreach_log_id_revision_number_key.

WHAT THE ERROR MEANS. The draft_revisions table has a unique constraint on the pair outreach_log_id and revision_number. Something tried to insert a revision whose number already exists for that touch.

THE THIRD SCREENSHOT SHOWS THE THING WORTH CHASING: TWO GREEN DRAFT APPROVED TOASTS, stacked, for one card, next to the one error. Two successes and one failure from a single approval is not one write going wrong, it is the approval running more than once. If it fires twice, both runs work out the same next revision number, the first insert takes it and the second collides. That explains the error without needing anything else to be wrong, and it is the first thing to check.

THE OTHER CANDIDATE, STILL OPEN. All three failing cards read PREVIOUS DRAFTS 1. If the next revision number is computed from the count of PREVIOUS drafts and ignores the current one, it lands on a number already in use and collides every time from the first revision onward. That would also fit.

HOW TO TELL THEM APART, CHEAPLY. Look at the server log for one approval click: two requests means the double fire, one request means the number is being computed wrong. Then approve a draft that has never been revised, PREVIOUS DRAFTS absent. If that one fails too, it is the double fire. If it succeeds, it is the count.

EITHER WAY THE WRITE HAS TO BE SAFE TO REPEAT, which is the real fix and is the same for both causes.

THE CARDS HAVE LITTLE ELSE IN COMMON. Largo is French, P1, five touches, message 1 of 3. Electronic Partner is German, P0, one touch, message 1 of 3. topi is German, P1, two touches, message 2 of 3. So it is not the language, the priority, the sequence position or the depth of the history.

NOT LOST APPROVALS, CORRECTING MY FIRST READ. The tab counters balance at every step: 74 pending, 7 approved, 17 rejected, then 69, 10, 19, then 64, 13, 21, then 63, 14, 21. Every drop out of Pending Review is matched by the rise into Approved and Rejected. The status change IS saving. What fails is the revision snapshot written alongside it.

CONFIRM IT IN A MINUTE: open the Approved tab and look for Gandon, Baumann and Coen. All three there means nothing has been lost and this is a false alarm over a real bug. Any missing and it is worse than described.

WHAT IS NEEDED

1. Make approve idempotent. Disable the button on submit, and make the write safe to run twice, so a repeat updates rather than inserts.

2. Stop computing the revision number in application code. Assign it inside the insert statement itself, or drop the column and order revisions by timestamp, keeping the unique constraint only as a guard.

3. Ask whether approving should write a revision at all. If the text has not changed, approving is a status change on the revision that already exists. If every approval forks a new row, the table grows for nothing and every approval is another chance to collide.

4. A failed write must never show a success toast, and a success must never show an error toast. Right now both appear together and neither can be trusted.

5. Decide what a half completed approval leaves behind. If the status saves and the revision does not, the revision history is missing the version that was approved, which is the one that matters when asking later what exactly Oliver signed off.$il$,
$il$THIS IS NOT THE SAME FAULT AS i070 AND i082, and it would be easy to file it with them. Those return unauthorized, which is a credential or token problem. This is the database refusing a write because of a constraint, which is a schema and code problem. Different fix. If they get bundled together, the constraint will still be there after the token is renewed.

IT IS RELATED TO i085 BUT NOT THE SAME. There, the approvals had saved and a filter contradiction made them invisible. Here an approval genuinely did not save. Both leave Oliver asking the same question, did that approval stick, which is why the honest toast in point 3 matters more than either fix taken alone.

THE TIMING MAKES IT WORSE. Send is broken, see i082, so the approved pile is where everything is accumulating. If approvals are also failing silently, that pile is not merely unsent, it is incomplete, and nobody knows by how much.

WHILE IT IS BROKEN. The tab counter is the only trustworthy signal on the screen, because it comes from the database rather than from the toast. Approved read 10 at the time of the screenshot. If Oliver approves ten more and it reaches 20, they all landed. If it lags, some did not.

SEEN ON THE SAME CARD, SEPARATELY. The connection request note to Frédéric is logged twice, two touch rows T503 and T817 both dated 5 Aug 26, and the conversation shows the same text twice as two Sent messages. That is recorded on i053, which now has two instances rather than one.$il$);
