insert into public.improvements (team_id, item_key, title, category, owner, priority, status, raised_by, created_at, build_wave, blocks, blocked_by, done_by, done_at, pinned, hidden, seen_by, detail, notes)
values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i097$il$, $il$Approve should queue and drip, Send now should mean now, on LinkedIn and on email$il$, $il$Outreach & drafting$il$, $il$Brad$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-17T10:45:00.000Z'::timestamptz, 6, array[$il$i107$il$]::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$TWO BUTTONS, TWO MEANINGS, AND RIGHT NOW THEY BOTH MEAN THE SAME THING.

APPROVE puts the message in a send queue. The queue releases it later, at an irregular interval, inside the day's allowance for that channel. Oliver approves a batch in one sitting and the system spreads it out.

SEND NOW sends it immediately, straight past the queue. That is what the button says and it should be what it does.

SAME MECHANISM FOR EMAIL. Different limits and different reasons, deliverability and warm-up rather than account safety, but one queue and one set of controls, not two systems.

WHAT THE QUEUE NEEDS

1. A DAILY BUDGET PER CHANNEL, not one number for everything. Connection requests, currently throttled at twenty, see i072. DMs. InMail, which is a credit count rather than a daily cap. Email. The queue stops when a channel's budget is spent and says so, rather than trying and failing.

2. IRREGULAR GAPS, NOT A FIXED CADENCE. A message every eleven minutes on the dot is a more obvious pattern than sending them all at once. Randomise the interval, and keep it inside working hours in the recipient's own country, weekdays, nothing at three in the morning.

3. ORDER BY VALUE, BECAUSE THE BUDGET RUNS OUT. If twenty of forty approved requests go today, they should be the best twenty. That is the same argument as i071, score first and spend from the top, applied at the moment of sending rather than the moment of sourcing.

4. VISIBLE AND REVERSIBLE. A Queued state on the touch, the time it is expected to go, and the ability to pull it back before it does. Approving forty messages that then leak out over three days is only safe if Oliver can see the queue and cancel from it.

5. SEND NOW OVERRIDES THE PACING, NEVER THE GUARDS. Do not contact, cooldown, the market rules on cold email in i089, the duplicate approach guard in i004: those still apply. Now means skip the queue, not skip the checks.

6. A FAILED SEND STAYS IN THE QUEUE AND RETRIES. It must never be dropped quietly. This matters more than usual given i082: an outage during a queued run would otherwise lose the whole batch with nothing on screen to say so.

7. THE BUDGET SPLITS BY PRODUCT. Pier Protect and Ticketplan draw on the same LinkedIn account and the same daily twenty. Oliver has to be able to say how much of today goes to each. That is i039, and it lands in this queue.$il$,
$il$WHY THIS IS THE RIGHT SHAPE. Approving and sending are two different decisions and they have been collapsed into one. Approving is a judgement about the message: is this good enough to go. Sending is a question about timing and budget: should it go now, and is there room today. Separating them is what makes it possible to review forty drafts in one sitting, which is how Oliver actually works, without sending forty messages in one sitting, which is how accounts get restricted.

IT IS ALSO THE ONLY WAY THE TWENTY A DAY BECOMES MANAGEABLE. At the moment the allowance is enforced by LinkedIn refusing, which is the worst place to find out, see i071. A queue that knows the budget turns a refusal into a schedule.

WHAT TO BE CAREFUL OF. An approved message goes stale. i085 already asks for the age of an approved draft to be visible; with a queue it matters more, because the delay is deliberate and could run to days when a backlog is draining. The queue should refuse to release anything older than a set age and put it back for a re-read instead. A message that opens on something that has since moved is worse than a message sent a day late.

AND THE QUEUE MUST NOT BECOME A PLACE THINGS GO MISSING. Every queued item needs to be reachable from the contact, not only from a queue screen, so that opening a contact shows there is something pending for them. Otherwise it is a second hidden pile next to the approved one.

DEPENDENCY. Nothing here can be tested until send works at all, i082 and i070. Worth building the queue behind it rather than in front of it, because a queue over a broken send will simply accumulate silently, which is the failure mode in point 6.

ONE OPEN QUESTION FOR BRAD. Does the queue release on a server side job or only while the app is open. It has to be server side, otherwise the spread only happens while Oliver has the tab open, which defeats the point. Worth confirming early because it decides where this gets built.$il$);

insert into public.improvements (team_id, item_key, title, category, owner, priority, status, raised_by, created_at, build_wave, blocks, blocked_by, done_by, done_at, pinned, hidden, seen_by, detail, notes)
values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i098$il$, $il$Drafts carry typos and grammar errors in both German and English, and the lint does not check for them$il$, $il$Outreach & drafting$il$, $il$Both$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Claude$il$, '2026-09-17T11:05:00.000Z'::timestamptz, 4, '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${"Brad": "2026-09-18T09:03:25.680Z"}$il$::jsonb,
$il$The draft to Hartmut Baumann, Director Purchasing at Electronic Partner, P675, a German P0 contact. LINT 100.

WHAT IS IN IT
  unsere monatliches Versicherungsabo. Abo is neuter, so it is unser monatliches Versicherungsabo.
  Ich bin dabei Partnerschaften ... auszubauen. The infinitive clause needs a comma: Ich bin dabei, Partnerschaften ... auszubauen.
  idR. for in der Regel.
  im vgl. zu for im Vergleich zu, and lower case where it is a noun.
  standard "opt-in" Produkten, with an English term in straight quotes and a lower case adjective where German wants Standard.
  A double space after the closing bracket.

IT IS NOT ONE DRAFT. The German exit message to Hermann-Wilhelm Wantia on the same day reads für unsere partner, lower case, in a language that capitalises every noun. Two German drafts on one morning, both with basic errors.

WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS. This is a cold first message to a purchasing director in Germany. Abbreviations like idR. and vgl. belong in an internal note, not in a first approach, and a wrong article is the kind of thing a German reader registers immediately even if they could not say why. The whole point of the opener is to read as though a person who knows the market wrote it.

WHAT IS NEEDED
  A language correctness pass on every draft before it reaches the review queue, in the language the draft is written in, not only in English.
  A ban on abbreviations in outbound. Write in der Regel, im Vergleich zu, and so on, in full.
  German nouns capitalised, which is mechanical and catchable.
  And the lint number must reflect it. A draft with a grammar error should not read 100.$il$,
$il$FOURTH AND FIFTH SIGHTINGS, AND ONE OF THEM HAS ALREADY GONE OUT. On the Leonard Coen card at topi, P707, 17 Sep. The pending draft reads für ähnliche vermieter, lower case, and Ich teile da gerne auch mal einen Case Study, where Studie is feminine so it is eine Case Study. Worse, the DM ALREADY SENT on 2 September reads Wie du weißt sind abo Modelle kommerziell sehr stark: Abo-Modelle takes a capital and a hyphen, and the clause needs a comma after weißt.

THAT CHANGES THE URGENCY. Every earlier example was sitting in the review queue where Oliver could still catch it. This one went to a Vice President of Strategic Partnerships in Germany with two errors in one sentence. The problem is already in the record of what Pier has said, not only in what it is about to say. Worth a sweep of what has been sent in German, not just what is queued.

THIS IS A DIFFERENT GAP FROM i084 AND THE TWO SHOULD NOT BE MERGED. There, guardrails were displayed and then not checked against the text. Here, nothing is checking the text for correctness in the first place. Both surface as LINT 100 on a draft that should not have scored 100, which is exactly why the score has to say what it actually tested, as i084 argues.

IT IS ALSO CHEAP TO FIX RELATIVE TO THE REST. Capitalisation and a fixed list of banned abbreviations are mechanical checks. Article agreement is not, but a second model pass in the draft language would catch all of it, and it can run once at generation rather than on every keystroke.

WORTH A LOOK AT WHERE THE GERMAN COMES FROM. If the drafts are being generated in English and translated, or generated by a model prompted in English, that would explain both the article slips and the English terms surviving in the text. If so the fix is upstream of any lint rule: prompt and generate in the target language.

AND IT COMPOUNDS WITH i037. If line breaks are already being lost somewhere between the draft and the send, a message that is both badly broken up and grammatically off is a poor first impression twice over.$il$);
